Chapter: RocFoldApplyChain

  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Three spellings of one computation, isolating COMPILER-40. All three must
 answer 10. The variants differ ONLY in whether the lambda reaches its
 arguments as a literal in function position, which is the single condition
 for `lower-apply-lambda-chain` (`codex/compiler/IR/Lowering.codex`) to run.

 immediate  a lambda LITERAL applied in the same expression that builds it.
            This is the only variant that enters lower-apply-lambda-chain,
            and it is the shape roc-fold-sum uses.
 letbound   the same lambda bound by a let first, then applied by name.
            Enters the ordinary apply path.
 named      a top-level definition applied by name. Ordinary apply path.

 If immediate answers a large pointer-shaped number and the other two
 answer 10, the defect is confined to lower-apply-lambda-chain and the
 expected-or-recorded-ty span it consults, which is what CL 20706 changed.
 If all three are wrong the defect is in the shared apply path instead and
 20706 is not the whole story.

Section: Functions

  fold-loop : List Integer, Integer, (Integer, Integer -> Integer), Integer -> Integer
  fold-loop (xs) (acc) (step) (i) =
   if i >= list-length xs then acc
   else fold-loop xs (step acc (list-at xs i)) step (i + 1)

  named-driver : List Integer, Integer, (Integer, Integer -> Integer) -> Integer
  named-driver (xs) (base) (step) = fold-loop xs base step 0

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   let immediate = (\xs base step -> fold-loop xs base step 0) [1, 2, 3, 4] 0 (\acc x -> acc + x)
   in let driver = \xs base step -> fold-loop xs base step 0
   in let letbound = driver [1, 2, 3, 4] 0 (\acc x -> acc + x)
   in let named = named-driver [1, 2, 3, 4] 0 (\acc x -> acc + x)
   in act
     print-line-uni ("immediate : " & show immediate)
     print-line-uni ("letbound  : " & show letbound)
     print-line-uni ("named     : " & show named)
   end
  end

